&lt;h1&gt;


  BuildInPublic #PWA #React #WebDevelopment
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Ask Claude Code, Codex or Cursor for "a hotel date picker with a price under each night" and you will get a calendar written from zero. It renders. It usually misses the details a mature component already handles: check-out on a night that is booked by the next guest, a minimum stay, the total under the selected range, keyboard navigation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We built Clay UI so the agent looks in established component libraries first, then adapts real code to your project.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Demo videos and the site: &lt;a href="https://clayui.behavio.one" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://clayui.behavio.one&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What the agent does with it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Install the skill in a project:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;npx @behavio1/clayui init
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Then ask in plain words, in any language. The skill tells the agent to translate the request into a concrete English search and run the CLI:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;clayui search &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"date range picker booking nightly price"&lt;/span&gt; --available-only --json
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;For that request the top three results are:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;kokonut/stay-picker     A date range picker for booking stays. Every night shows its price...
kokonut/team-selector   Seat picker with a scrubbable number, generated avatars and a price line
shadcn/calendar         Select a date or a range of dates (react-day-picker)
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The agent shows you the options with preview links. You pick one, and it downloads the source:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;clayui get kokonut/stay-picker --out .clayui-examples --json
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The folder holds the component files exactly as the library publishes them, the usage notes from its docs, and a &lt;code&gt;clayui.json&lt;/code&gt;. Here is part of the real one:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"id"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"kokonut/stay-picker"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"sourceUrl"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kokonut-labs/kokonutui/main/public/r/stay-picker.json"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"sourceRef"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"main"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"license"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"MIT"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"dependencies"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"lucide-react"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"motion"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"gaps"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"local-imports-missing"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"missingLocalImports"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"file"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"components/kokonutui/stay-picker.tsx"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"import"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"@/lib/utils"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;"files"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"components/kokonutui/stay-picker.tsx"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;It also stores a SHA-256 for every file. The gap here is the usual &lt;code&gt;cn&lt;/code&gt; helper from &lt;code&gt;@/lib/utils&lt;/code&gt;, which most shadcn-style projects already have. The agent sees it and either reuses yours or adds it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The agent then rewrites the example into your codebase: your tokens, your data, your naming. Clay UI never runs &lt;code&gt;npm install&lt;/code&gt; and writes only into the examples folder.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Where the components come from
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;shadcn/ui, Radix, Base UI, Sonner, Vaul, cmdk, NumberFlow, Beautiful UI, RareUI, Magic UI, Aceternity UI, 21st.dev and Kokonut UI. The CLI keeps a local index of them and refreshes it from each library's registry or repository.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Two decisions that mattered
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Be honest about what is missing.&lt;/strong&gt; A component that imports &lt;code&gt;../utils/foo&lt;/code&gt; you did not get is not a working example. &lt;code&gt;get&lt;/code&gt; resolves every relative and alias import against the delivered files and reports the ones that are missing as &lt;code&gt;local-imports-missing&lt;/code&gt;. Paid or deleted items exit with code 3 instead of writing a half-empty folder. The agent reads those gaps before it starts adapting.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Search without a key, better with one.&lt;/strong&gt; Out of the box the search is BM25 over names, descriptions and synonyms, with no account needed. Set a Gemini API key and it adds semantic search with &lt;code&gt;gemini-embedding-001&lt;/code&gt;, which handles loose requests like "this checkout feels cheap" much better. Query embeddings are cached for 30 days.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Try it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Install: &lt;code&gt;npx @behavio1/clayui init&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Code and docs (MIT): &lt;a href="https://github.com/behavio1/clay-ui" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://github.com/behavio1/clay-ui&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;How we measure search quality: &lt;a href="https://github.com/behavio1/clay-ui/blob/main/docs/search-tuning.md" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://github.com/behavio1/clay-ui/blob/main/docs/search-tuning.md&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Short demos: &lt;a href="https://www.youtube.com/@BehavioOne" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://www.youtube.com/@BehavioOne&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The CLI is a small Rust binary, shipped through npm for macOS, Linux and Windows. If a search returns the wrong component for your request, open an issue with the query. That is the most useful feedback we can get.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Disclosure: this account belongs to Behavio One, the team that built Clay UI.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It initially re-render rather than re-rendering of everytime and you control whether it runs again using the dependency array.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;eg:&lt;br&gt;
useEffect(() =&amp;gt; {&lt;br&gt;
  console.log("Runs only once when the component first loads");&lt;br&gt;
}, []);&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;-&amp;gt; when specific values change: Put those variables inside the array.&lt;br&gt;
eg:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;useEffect(() =&amp;gt; {&lt;br&gt;
  console.log("Runs on first load, and ONLY when 'userId' changes");&lt;br&gt;
}, [userId]);&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Client-side Module Federation has a well-known weakness, the browser downloads a shell, then fetches remotes, then renders. Users stare at a blank page, and crawlers may see very little. For content-heavy or SEO-sensitive products, that's a deal-breaker, and it's why teams keep asking the same question, &lt;em&gt;can we get independently deployed micro-frontends **and&lt;/em&gt;* server-rendered HTML?*&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The answer is yes, with caveats. Module Federation supports server-side rendering, and the same model that works in the browser transfers to Node, a host fetches code from remotes at runtime and renders it on the server. But SSR raises the stakes. You now have two runtimes to keep consistent, a network dependency inside your render path, and a security boundary you didn't have before.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This guide explains how the pieces fit, shows a working configuration pattern, and is candid about where the ecosystem is solid and where it isn't.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  How Module Federation Works with SSR
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In client-only federation, a remote publishes a &lt;code&gt;remoteEntry.js&lt;/code&gt;, and the browser loads it on demand. With SSR, each application builds &lt;strong&gt;twice&lt;/strong&gt;, a browser bundle and a server bundle, each with its own federation entry.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;Request ──► Host server (Node)
              │  1. fetch remote's SERVER entry (cached)
              │  2. execute remote code, render to HTML stream
              ▼
          HTML response ──► Browser
                              │  3. load remote's CLIENT entry
                              ▼
                          Hydration
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Conceptually, the remotes act as static asset servers, the host's server pulls their server-side code and executes it in the same Node process that renders the page. That's what makes independent deployment possible, ship a new remote and the host picks it up without a rebuild, and it's also the source of most SSR-specific risk.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 1 - Build the Remote Twice
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The remote exposes a component and produces a server entry alongside the client one. A server federation config typically targets a Node environment and uses the &lt;code&gt;@module-federation/node&lt;/code&gt; runtime plugin:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;npm &lt;span class="nb"&gt;install&lt;/span&gt; @module-federation/enhanced @module-federation/node
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;





&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// remote/webpack.server.js&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ModuleFederationPlugin&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;require&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;@module-federation/enhanced/webpack&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nx"&gt;module&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;exports&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;target&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;async-node&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;output&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;__dirname&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;/dist/server&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;libraryTarget&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;commonjs-module&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;plugins&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;ModuleFederationPlugin&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="na"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;productApp&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="na"&gt;filename&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;remoteEntry.js&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="na"&gt;library&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;commonjs-module&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="na"&gt;exposes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;./ProductList&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;./src/ProductList&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="na"&gt;runtimePlugins&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;require&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;resolve&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;@module-federation/node/runtimePlugin&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)],&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="na"&gt;shared&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;react&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;singleton&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;react-dom&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;singleton&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}),&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;],&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The client config mirrors our earlier micro-frontend setup (&lt;code&gt;target: 'web'&lt;/code&gt;, output to &lt;code&gt;dist/client&lt;/code&gt;). Serve both directories from the remote's static host so each has its own entry URL, for example &lt;code&gt;/server/remoteEntry.js&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;/client/remoteEntry.js&lt;/code&gt;. Config details vary by version, so treat this as a pattern and confirm option names in the current docs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 2 - Load Remotes at Runtime on the Host
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On the host's server, use the federation runtime to register and load remotes dynamically rather than hardcoding them at build time:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// host/server/federation.ts&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;init&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;loadRemote&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;@module-federation/enhanced/runtime&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nf"&gt;init&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;host&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;remotes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;productApp&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;entry&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;process&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;env&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;PRODUCT_APP_URL&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;/server/remoteEntry.js`&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;],&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;loadProductList&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;mod&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;loadRemote&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;React&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;ComponentType&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kr"&gt;any&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;productApp/ProductList&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;mod&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;?.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;??&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Then render with a timeout and a fallback so a slow remote can't hang your page:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// host/server/render.tsx&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;renderToPipeableStream&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;react-dom/server&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;renderPage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;res&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;props&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;ProductList&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;withTimeout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;loadProductList&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;800&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;catch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;pipe&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;renderToPipeableStream&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;Layout&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;ProductList&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;ProductList&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{...&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;props&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sr"&gt;/&amp;gt; : &amp;lt;ProductListSkeleton /&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;/Layout&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="err"&gt;,
&lt;/span&gt;    &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;onShellReady&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;pipe&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;res&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;If the remote can't be loaded in time, the server returns the shell with a skeleton, and the browser loads the client entry and fills it in. Users get a degraded-but-working page instead of an error.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 3 - Hydration Without Mismatches
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;SSR only pays off if hydration succeeds. Three rules prevent most mismatches:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Keep server and client versions identical.&lt;/strong&gt; The HTML produced by the remote's server build must match what its client build renders. Deploy both bundles together, under the same version identifier.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Serialize the data you rendered with.&lt;/strong&gt; Embed the props or fetched data in the HTML and reuse them on the client, so the first client render doesn't refetch and produce different output.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Share singletons on both sides.&lt;/strong&gt; React must be a single instance in both the server and client graphs. Duplicate React copies cause hook errors that only appear in production.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Step 4 - Resilience in the Render Path
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Putting a network call inside server rendering changes your failure model. Plan for it:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Cache remote entries.&lt;/strong&gt; Don't refetch &lt;code&gt;remoteEntry.js&lt;/code&gt; on every request. Cache in memory with a short TTL, and refresh in the background.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Pin to immutable URLs.&lt;/strong&gt; Use content-hashed or versioned paths for server entries so a half-finished deploy can't serve mismatched files.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Set strict timeouts and circuit breakers.&lt;/strong&gt; After repeated failures, stop attempting the remote for a cooldown period and serve the fallback.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Monitor per-remote render time.&lt;/strong&gt; Add remote name and duration to your traces so a slow remote is identifiable in minutes. Our distributed tracing guide covers the instrumentation.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Security: The Remote Is Now Code on Your Server
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is the part teams underestimate. In client federation, a compromised remote runs in the user's browser. With SSR federation, it runs &lt;strong&gt;inside your server process&lt;/strong&gt;, with access to its environment, network, and secrets.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Treat remote entries as a supply-chain dependency:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Load only from origins you control, over TLS.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Never pass secrets or full request objects into remote components.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Run the host's render process with least privilege and no unnecessary credentials in its environment.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Where possible, verify integrity of fetched entries (hashes or signed manifests) before execution.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you wouldn't &lt;code&gt;npm install&lt;/code&gt; a package from that source, don't federate from it on the server.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Framework and Tooling Reality Check
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The ecosystem has shifted, and this matters if you're planning around a specific framework:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Stack&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Federation + SSR status&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Webpack / Rspack with &lt;code&gt;@module-federation/enhanced&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Supported, server builds use the Node runtime plugin&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Modern.js, Rsbuild plugin&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Actively supported by the Module Federation project&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Vite via &lt;code&gt;@module-federation/vite&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Available, Node/SSR consumption has had rough edges, so test carefully&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Angular with Nx&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Supported historically, Nx is moving server builds toward the standard federation plugin, so check migration notes for your version&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Next.js&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;@module-federation/nextjs-mf&lt;/code&gt; supports the Pages Router only, and the Module Federation team announced end of support in late 2024&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Next.js row deserves emphasis. If your plan depends on federating Next.js applications, verify current status before committing, and have an alternative ready, separate deployments composed at the edge or gateway, or a framework with first-class federation support.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  When Not to Use Federation with SSR
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Module Federation adds runtime coupling, versioning complexity, and debugging overhead. Consider simpler options first:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;A monorepo with a shared component library&lt;/strong&gt; gives you reuse without runtime composition.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Server-side HTML composition&lt;/strong&gt; (a gateway stitching fragments from independent services) avoids executing remote code in your renderer.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Route-level separation&lt;/strong&gt; (each team owns whole pages on separate deployments) avoids composition entirely.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Federation with SSR earns its complexity when multiple teams must deploy independently &lt;em&gt;and&lt;/em&gt; share a rendered page, with real SEO or first-paint requirements.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Conclusion
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Combining Module Federation with SSR closes the biggest gap in client-only micro-frontends, first paint and crawlability. The pattern is sound, build each application for browser and server, load remotes at runtime, hydrate with matching versions, but it moves a network dependency and untrusted-code risk into your render path.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The practical path forward is to pilot with one remote on a well-supported toolchain, add timeouts, caching, and fallbacks from day one, and treat server-side remotes as part of your supply chain. Where the framework support isn't there, choose a simpler composition strategy rather than fighting the tooling. The future of micro-frontends is not one pattern, it's choosing the lightest composition that meets your team's independence and performance needs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Running federation with SSR in production on Rspack, Modern.js, or Webpack? Share which toolchain you chose and what broke first in the comments.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


  MicroFrontends #ModuleFederation #SSR #ServerSideRendering #React
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Picture a tooltip that should sit above a button. For one frame it shows up at the top of the box, then it jumps into place.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The measurement was right. It ran too late. &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; let the browser paint the first, wrong position.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The mental model: before the curtain, or after
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;React updates the DOM, then the browser paints what the user sees. There is a gap between those two, like the moment before a curtain opens.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;useLayoutEffect&lt;/code&gt; runs in that gap, while the curtain is closed. &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; runs after the curtain opens, once the user can already see the frame.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Both take the same arguments: a setup function, a dependency list, and an optional cleanup. The only difference is &lt;strong&gt;when&lt;/strong&gt; the setup runs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fbitlyst.blog%2Fassets%2Fuseeffect-layout-curtain.jpg" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fbitlyst.blog%2Fassets%2Fuseeffect-layout-curtain.jpg" alt="AI-generated illustration. A fix made while the curtain is closed never becomes a flash. A fix made after it opens does." width="800" height="400"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;One update, from commit to paint&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;1. React writes the DOM  — The new elements are in the document
2. useLayoutEffect       — Measure and correct, still invisible
3. Browser paints        — This is the frame the user sees
4. useEffect             — Fetch, subscribe, log
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;The user only sees the frame after useLayoutEffect has finished.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  How it works
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; is the one for work that talks to the outside world: a fetch, a subscription, a timer, a log line. The browser paints first, so the effect doesn't hold the frame up. The user sees the new UI, then the effect runs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;useLayoutEffect&lt;/code&gt; is for the case where that first paint would be a lie. You need a number the browser only knows after layout, such as a height from &lt;code&gt;getBoundingClientRect&lt;/code&gt;, and you need to change what's on screen with it.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;height&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;tipRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;getBoundingClientRect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setTop&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;buttonBottom&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;height&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// one frame at top: 0, then it jumps&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;buttonBottom&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Swap in &lt;code&gt;useLayoutEffect&lt;/code&gt; and that &lt;code&gt;setTop&lt;/code&gt; is flushed &lt;strong&gt;before&lt;/strong&gt; the browser paints. React may render the tooltip twice, first at &lt;code&gt;top: 0&lt;/code&gt; and then at the measured spot. The user only sees the second render.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That correction is also the cost. The code inside &lt;code&gt;useLayoutEffect&lt;/code&gt;, and any state update it schedules, &lt;strong&gt;blocks the paint&lt;/strong&gt;. A slow one delays the frame. &lt;a href="https://react.dev/reference/react/useLayoutEffect" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;The React docs&lt;/a&gt; say to prefer &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; unless that blocking is the point.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The dependency list works the same on both. It still decides when the effect re-runs, and a stale value is still stale. Timing doesn't change that. The rules are in &lt;a href="https://bitlyst.blog/p/react-useeffect-dependencies" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;useEffect dependencies&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  In practice
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Start with &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt;. Move to &lt;code&gt;useLayoutEffect&lt;/code&gt; only after you've seen a flash.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The flash shows up when you &lt;strong&gt;read layout and then write the screen&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A tooltip or menu that must flip above or below, depending on the space left.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A textarea whose height follows its content.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Scrolling or focusing an element before the user sees it in the wrong place.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Leave fetching, subscriptions, and logging on &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt;. They don't change the frame that's about to paint. Running them in &lt;code&gt;useLayoutEffect&lt;/code&gt; makes that paint wait for work the user didn't need to wait for.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Honest stakes:&lt;/strong&gt; most components never measure the DOM. You'll want &lt;code&gt;useLayoutEffect&lt;/code&gt; a handful of times, usually on something floating. Reaching for it to be safe makes the first paint slower. Wait until something flickers.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Say it in an interview
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;"useEffect and useLayoutEffect take the same arguments. useLayoutEffect runs after React commits DOM changes and before the browser paints, so you can measure layout and correct it without a visible flash. useEffect runs after the paint, so it doesn't block the frame. State updates inside useLayoutEffect are flushed before that paint. Default to useEffect, and switch only when the user would otherwise see one wrong frame."&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Cheat sheet
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;useEffect&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;useLayoutEffect&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;When it runs&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;After the browser paints&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;After the DOM update, before the paint&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Blocks the paint?&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;No&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Yes&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Use it for&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Fetch, subscribe, log&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Measure layout, then fix what the user would see&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A state update inside&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Shows up on a later frame&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Flushed before the paint&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;The default&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;This one&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Only after you've seen a flash&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  TL;DR
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;useLayoutEffect&lt;/code&gt; runs before the paint. &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt; runs after it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The layout version can measure the DOM and correct it before anyone sees the wrong frame.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;That correction blocks the paint, so a slow one delays the frame.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Start with &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt;. Switch when something flickers.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  Going deeper
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Advanced and optional. Everything above is enough to explain this in an interview. This part is for the curious: full numbers, edge cases and what's still unproven.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  What "flushed before paint" means
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The tooltip renders once with &lt;code&gt;top: 0&lt;/code&gt;. The effect measures it, and &lt;code&gt;setTop&lt;/code&gt; asks for another render. Inside &lt;code&gt;useLayoutEffect&lt;/code&gt;, React does that second render, and the DOM update that follows, before the browser paints. Two commits, one frame the user sees.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Inside &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt;, React lets the browser paint the &lt;code&gt;top: 0&lt;/code&gt; frame first, then processes the update. That's the flash. On a fast machine you may not catch it. The React docs reproduce it by slowing the render down. The extra frame is still there when you don't.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Writing the style on the node itself, &lt;code&gt;tipRef.current.style.top = ...&lt;/code&gt;, also lands before the paint, and it skips the second render. Setting state is the usual fix. It costs that extra render, still hidden behind the curtain.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  A state update there runs the other effects early
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One caveat from the React docs: if &lt;code&gt;useLayoutEffect&lt;/code&gt; sets state, React runs the remaining effects immediately, including &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt;. The paint is still waiting. So "useEffect runs after the paint" is the normal case. A layout effect that sets state can pull a passive effect forward into the same gap.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  The server warning
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Effects don't run during server rendering. There is no layout to measure, and React warns that &lt;code&gt;useLayoutEffect&lt;/code&gt; does nothing on the server.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A Next.js client component is still pre-rendered to HTML. A tooltip that depends on a measurement will be in the wrong place in that HTML, then jump once JavaScript runs. If showing the first result is acceptable, use &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt;. If the component only makes sense in the browser, don't render it on the server: keep a &lt;code&gt;mounted&lt;/code&gt; flag that starts &lt;code&gt;false&lt;/code&gt; and flips to &lt;code&gt;true&lt;/code&gt; inside a &lt;code&gt;useEffect&lt;/code&gt;, and render the measured UI only after that.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Strict Mode runs the setup twice, in development
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;With Strict Mode, React runs an extra setup and cleanup before the real setup, in development only. Both hooks do this. If the double run breaks something, the cleanup isn't undoing the setup. &lt;a href="https://bitlyst.blog/p/react-useeffect-dependencies" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;useEffect dependencies&lt;/a&gt; walks through that cycle.&lt;/p&gt;

